# Architecture

Follow the repository’s actual framework and conventions once they exist. Until then, establish clear layers and do not invent a second architecture alongside the first.

## Layers

Keep these boundaries separate:

| Layer | Responsibility |
|-------|----------------|
| UI | Presentation, local UI state, accessibility, loading/empty/error states |
| Application / services | Use-cases, orchestration, authorization checks |
| Domain | Financial formulas, inventory rules, matching policies |
| Repositories | Persistence contracts; no business formulas |
| Database | Prisma/SQL access only inside repositories (or designated data layer) |

- UI must not contain authoritative financial calculations.
- Repositories must not call UI or embed fee/profit formulas.
- Prefer feature modules with clear public exports over deep cross-feature imports.

## Contracts and validation

- Define typed service and repository interfaces (inputs/outputs explicit).
- Share API request/response types between client and server where the stack allows; keep a single source of truth.
- Centralize validation (e.g. Zod schemas) and map failures to consistent error shapes.
- Validate all untrusted input on the server; client validation is UX only.

## Structure guidelines

- Reuse shared UI primitives; keep feature-specific components inside the feature.
- Avoid oversized components: split when a file mixes fetching, domain math, and dense presentation.
- Avoid unnecessary abstraction (no generic “BaseManager” layers without a second real use).
- Avoid circular dependencies; depend inward toward domain, not outward toward UI.
- Use async safely: handle cancellation/race conditions on navigations; never leave unhandled rejections in services.

## Example boundary

```typescript
// ✅ UI calls a service; service uses domain + repository
const summary = await profitService.getWorkspaceSummary(workspaceId, range);

// ❌ UI computes contribution profit with raw JS numbers
const profit = order.total - order.cogs - order.fees;
```
